1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,523
September 11th, 2001. Nine thirty in the morning.

2
00:00:03,523 --> 00:00:08,708
Thirty five thousand feet over Ohio, Tom Burnett's phone rings in the cabin.

3
00:00:08,708 --> 00:00:12,297
It's his wife. She has something to tell him.

4
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:18,305
Planes have hit the World Trade Center, she says. He already knows something she doesn't.

5
00:00:18,305 --> 00:00:20,904
His own flight has just been hijacked too.

6
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:29,895
In the next thirty minutes, forty four people will decide, together, to do something no hijacked passengers had ever done before.

7
00:00:29,895 --> 00:00:32,142
Rush the men holding the plane.

8
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:39,201
This is not a story about a bomb that went off, or a mechanical failure, or pilot error.

9
00:00:39,201 --> 00:00:43,487
This is a black box that recorded something almost nobody expects to hear.

10
00:00:43,487 --> 00:00:45,465
Ordinary people, choosing what happens next.

11
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:57,283
Here is the true story of United Airlines Flight 93, built entirely from the official investigation, the declassified cockpit transcript, and the words the people on board left behind.

12
00:00:58,433 --> 00:01:00,079
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

13
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,516
United 93. The passengers who fought back.

14
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:08,252
Chapter one. A forty-two minute delay.

15
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:16,458
United Airlines Flight 93 was scheduled to leave Newark, New Jersey for San Francisco at 8 o'clock that morning.

16
00:01:16,458 --> 00:01:19,430
A Boeing seven fifty-seven. Thirty-seven passengers, seven crew.

17
00:01:20,566 --> 00:01:23,283
Routine ground congestion held it at the gate.

18
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:27,358
It did not actually take off until eight forty-two, forty-two minutes late.

19
00:01:27,358 --> 00:01:31,093
That delay would matter more than anyone on board could know.

20
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:38,194
Among the passengers were four men traveling together, though they did not sit together.

21
00:01:38,194 --> 00:01:40,749
Seats 1B, 3C, 3D, and 6B.

22
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:45,372
The man in 1B, nearest the cockpit, was Ziad Jarrah.

23
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:56,939
According to the FBI and the nine eleven Commission, he had trained as a pilot at flight schools in Venice, Florida, after being radicalized in Germany and meeting Osama bin Laden in Afghanistan.

24
00:01:58,066 --> 00:02:07,621
The other three, Ahmed al-Haznawi, Ahmed al-Nami, and Saeed al-Ghamdi, were recruited through mosque networks in Saudi Arabia and trained at Al-Qaeda camps in Afghanistan.

25
00:02:07,621 --> 00:02:12,590
Their job was to subdue the crew and passengers, not fly the plane.

26
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:18,840
This was supposed to be a five-man team, like the other three flights hijacked that morning.

27
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,074
A fifth man never made it aboard.

28
00:02:21,074 --> 00:02:26,820
An immigration inspector in Orlando turned him away in August, suspicious of his cash and his one-way ticket.

29
00:02:27,966 --> 00:02:32,428
The nine eleven Commission's assessment is that Flight 93 was headed for the U.S.

30
00:02:32,428 --> 00:02:34,021
Capitol, or the White House.

31
00:02:34,021 --> 00:02:39,120
It could not determine which with certainty, because the flight never got close enough to either.

32
00:02:40,266 --> 00:02:42,617
Chapter two. Nine twenty-eight in the morning.

33
00:02:43,766 --> 00:02:49,546
By the time Flight 93 took off, two other hijacked planes had already struck the World Trade Center.

34
00:02:49,546 --> 00:02:53,400
United's own dispatcher sent a warning to the cockpit at nine twenty-four.

35
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:56,932
The captain didn't yet understand it was about his own flight.

36
00:02:58,066 --> 00:03:02,533
At nine twenty-eight, over eastern Ohio, at thirty-five thousand feet, it happened.

37
00:03:02,533 --> 00:03:06,628
Cleveland air traffic control heard a Mayday call, then a struggle.

38
00:03:06,628 --> 00:03:11,467
Then: get out of here, get out of here, get out of here.

39
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:18,248
Four minutes later, a hijacker keyed the cabin intercom, thinking he was speaking to the passengers.

40
00:03:18,248 --> 00:03:22,131
Instead, it went out over the radio, to every controller listening.

41
00:03:22,131 --> 00:03:24,956
We have a bomb on board. So, sit.

42
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:30,621
The FBI later found no trace of explosives at the crash site.

43
00:03:30,621 --> 00:03:34,766
The cockpit recorder never captured a gun being fired or mentioned.

44
00:03:34,766 --> 00:03:38,534
The investigation's conclusion: the bomb, almost certainly, was a bluff.

45
00:03:39,666 --> 00:03:42,252
Chapter three. Thirty-seven phone calls.

46
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:47,364
What happened next is why we know this story in such detail.

47
00:03:47,364 --> 00:03:54,633
Between about nine thirty-two and nine fifty-seven, at least ten passengers and two crew members placed thirty-seven phone calls from the cabin.

48
00:03:55,766 --> 00:04:03,581
And because those calls happened after the World Trade Center was already burning, the people on this plane learned something the hijackers never expected them to know.

49
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:06,764
What a hijacking like this was actually going to end with.

50
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:13,932
According to the Commission Report's own text, at least five of those calls described something else.

51
00:04:13,932 --> 00:04:15,063
Not fear. Intent.

52
00:04:15,063 --> 00:04:19,211
The passengers and surviving crew were already talking about fighting back.

53
00:04:20,333 --> 00:04:23,102
Chapter four. Voices from the cabin.

54
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:26,567
We know some of their names now.

55
00:04:26,567 --> 00:04:31,570
And thanks to the people who answered their calls, we know some of their words.

56
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:37,240
What you are about to hear is not a recording. Nothing from these calls was ever recorded.

57
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:42,910
These are voice recreations of documented, sourced accounts, so their words can be heard, not just read.

58
00:04:44,033 --> 00:04:49,074
Todd Beamer, in row 32, tried to call his family. The call never reached them.

59
00:04:49,074 --> 00:04:56,467
It was answered instead by a GTE Airfone supervisor named Lisa Jefferson, who stayed on the line with him for thirteen minutes.

60
00:04:57,599 --> 00:05:00,758
Are you guys ready? Okay. Let's roll.

61
00:05:01,899 --> 00:05:09,469
By Jefferson's own account, that is the last thing she heard him say, turning away from the phone toward the passengers seated near him.

62
00:05:09,469 --> 00:05:14,831
No recording exists. It is her memory of him, given to the FBI and repeated ever since.

63
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:19,151
Tom Burnett called his wife, Deena, four times that morning.

64
00:05:19,151 --> 00:05:22,974
In an earlier call, he told her a passenger had been stabbed.

65
00:05:22,974 --> 00:05:25,840
In a later one, he told her something else.

66
00:05:26,966 --> 00:05:32,694
We can't wait for the authorities. I don't know what they could do anyway. It's up to us.

67
00:05:32,694 --> 00:05:36,830
I think we can do it. Don't worry, we're going to do something.

68
00:05:37,966 --> 00:05:45,585
That wording comes from Deena Burnett's own account: her FBI interview, her book, and more than twenty years of consistent public retelling.

69
00:05:45,585 --> 00:05:50,087
It is the one version of his words that this documentary will use.

70
00:05:51,233 --> 00:05:54,033
Mark Bingham's call reached his aunt's house in California.

71
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:58,077
His mother, Alice Hoagland, happened to pick up the second time he called.

72
00:05:59,199 --> 00:06:03,265
This is Mark Bingham. I want to let you know I love you. I love you all.

73
00:06:03,265 --> 00:06:09,722
I'm on a flight from Newark to San Francisco and there are three guys who have taken over the plane, and they say they have a bomb.

74
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:12,871
Sandy Bradshaw was a flight attendant.

75
00:06:12,871 --> 00:06:18,551
She called her husband, Phil, and described boiling water in the galley to throw at the hijackers.

76
00:06:18,551 --> 00:06:25,233
Her closing line is the one verbatim passenger or crew quote written directly into the Commission Report's own main text.

77
00:06:26,366 --> 00:06:30,487
Everyone's running to first class. I've got to go. Bye.

78
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:33,892
CeeCee Lyles was also a flight attendant.

79
00:06:33,892 --> 00:06:37,119
Her first call went to her husband Lorne's answering machine.

80
00:06:38,266 --> 00:06:42,693
Baby, you have to listen to me carefully. I'm on a plane that's been hijacked.

81
00:06:42,693 --> 00:06:45,054
I want to tell you I love you.

82
00:06:45,054 --> 00:06:49,480
Please tell my children that I love them very much and I'm so sorry, babe.

83
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:53,907
I hope to be able to see your face again, baby. I love you. Goodbye.

84
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:58,943
She called him again eleven minutes later. This time he answered.

85
00:06:58,943 --> 00:07:04,986
By his account, they prayed together, and she told him: we're getting ready to do it now.

86
00:07:06,133 --> 00:07:09,833
Others made calls too. Lauren Grandcolas, calm, to her husband.

87
00:07:09,833 --> 00:07:11,684
Linda Gronlund, to her sister.

88
00:07:11,684 --> 00:07:19,455
Jeremy Glick, who asked his wife Lyzbeth if it was okay with her if he and the others rushed the hijackers.

89
00:07:20,599 --> 00:07:23,492
Edward Felt didn't call a loved one.

90
00:07:23,492 --> 00:07:30,518
He dialed 911 from an onboard bathroom, and reached a real emergency dispatcher in Westmoreland County, Pennsylvania.

91
00:07:30,518 --> 00:07:36,716
One of the only calls independently logged by a government agency, not a family's memory.

92
00:07:37,866 --> 00:07:39,668
Chapter five. The vote.

93
00:07:40,799 --> 00:07:46,926
At some point before nine fifty-seven, according to one of the phone calls, the passengers voted.

94
00:07:46,926 --> 00:07:51,137
Whether to rush the hijackers and try to retake the plane.

95
00:07:51,137 --> 00:07:54,200
The Commission's own words: they decided, and acted.

96
00:07:55,333 --> 00:08:00,863
We don't know exactly what that vote looked like. A show of hands, a shouted agreement.

97
00:08:00,863 --> 00:08:04,319
The Commission doesn't say, and this documentary won't invent it.

98
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:08,917
At nine fifty-seven in the morning, the passenger assault began.

99
00:08:08,917 --> 00:08:10,988
The Commission Report states it plainly.

100
00:08:10,988 --> 00:08:16,855
Several passengers had ended phone calls with the people they loved, in order to join the fight.

101
00:08:17,999 --> 00:08:20,272
Chapter six. Ninety seconds.

102
00:08:21,399 --> 00:08:25,929
What happened in the cockpit over the next six minutes comes from one source only.

103
00:08:25,929 --> 00:08:27,136
The cockpit voice recorder.

104
00:08:27,136 --> 00:08:31,665
And here is what you need to understand about it, before you hear a word.

105
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:37,935
That recording has been played publicly exactly once, for a jury, in 2006.

106
00:08:37,935 --> 00:08:41,095
It has never been released to the public.

107
00:08:41,095 --> 00:08:48,995
What follows is a transcript recreation — not the actual audio, which does not exist anywhere outside a federal courtroom.

108
00:08:50,133 --> 00:08:52,967
In the cockpit. If we don't we'll die!

109
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:56,311
Is that it? Shall we finish it off?

110
00:08:57,433 --> 00:09:01,053
No. Not yet. When they all come, we finish it off.

111
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:03,031
Roll it!

112
00:09:04,166 --> 00:09:07,057
That's a different line from Beamer's Let's roll.

113
00:09:07,057 --> 00:09:14,646
This one comes from the cockpit recorder itself, during the struggle, from a passenger the Commission was never able to identify.

114
00:09:14,646 --> 00:09:17,175
Two separate moments. Do not confuse them.

115
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:25,681
Jarrah rolled the plane sharply, side to side, then pitched the nose up and down, trying to throw the passengers off their feet.

116
00:09:25,681 --> 00:09:27,286
The assault did not stop.

117
00:09:28,433 --> 00:09:31,114
Allah is the greatest! Allah is the greatest!

118
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:34,041
Pull it down! Pull it down!

119
00:09:35,166 --> 00:09:37,991
The recording ends at ten-oh-three and eleven seconds.

120
00:09:37,991 --> 00:09:46,817
The aircraft rolled onto its back and dove into a reclaimed strip mine field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania, at more than five hundred miles an hour.

121
00:09:47,966 --> 00:09:51,663
All forty-four people aboard died. Thirty-three passengers. Seven crew. Four hijackers.

122
00:09:51,663 --> 00:09:54,015
No one on the ground was hurt.

123
00:09:54,015 --> 00:10:00,400
It is the only one of the four hijacked flights that morning where nobody outside the plane was killed.

124
00:10:01,533 --> 00:10:03,335
Chapter seven. Shanksville.

125
00:10:04,466 --> 00:10:09,769
The plane hit the ground at such speed and such an angle that there was no survivable wreckage.

126
00:10:09,769 --> 00:10:15,072
This was now a federal crime scene, led by the FBI, in a quiet corner of Somerset County.

127
00:10:16,199 --> 00:10:21,013
Identifying who had been aboard fell to the county coroner, Wallace Miller.

128
00:10:21,013 --> 00:10:26,628
The first sixteen victims were identified within two weeks, through fingerprints and dental records.

129
00:10:26,628 --> 00:10:28,633
The rest required DNA analysis.

130
00:10:29,766 --> 00:10:34,256
All forty-four people aboard, including all four hijackers, were eventually identified.

131
00:10:34,256 --> 00:10:39,562
Jarrah's remains were matched using a sample submitted by his girlfriend in Germany.

132
00:10:40,699 --> 00:10:45,836
Today, that field is the Flight 93 National Memorial, run by the National Park Service.

133
00:10:45,836 --> 00:10:53,368
It sits about twenty minutes' flying time from a Capitol building that, that morning, had no idea how close it had come.

134
00:10:54,499 --> 00:10:57,346
Chapter eight. The story Norad told.

135
00:10:58,466 --> 00:11:02,449
For years afterward, the U.S. military's own version of this story was simple.

136
00:11:02,449 --> 00:11:07,350
We were tracking Flight 93. We were ready to shoot it down if we had to.

137
00:11:07,350 --> 00:11:11,945
Here is what the nine eleven Commission's investigators actually found when they pulled the tapes.

138
00:11:13,066 --> 00:11:22,242
Three F-16s from a North Dakota Air National Guard unit stationed at Langley Air Force Base were scrambled at nine twenty-three, airborne by nine thirty.

139
00:11:22,242 --> 00:11:27,747
In 2003, Norad testified this was in response to Flight 93, or to American 77.

140
00:11:28,866 --> 00:11:31,312
The Commission's investigators subpoenaed the actual tapes.

141
00:11:31,312 --> 00:11:45,637
What they found was that those jets were scrambled because of a mistaken report that the first hijacked plane, American 11, which had already hit the World Trade Center thirty-eight minutes earlier, was somehow still in the air, headed for Washington.

142
00:11:46,766 --> 00:11:50,997
The fighters were never told the real threat was hijacked airliners.

143
00:11:50,997 --> 00:11:56,382
Their own lead pilot later said: I'm thinking cruise missile threat from the sea.

144
00:11:56,382 --> 00:11:57,920
Nobody told us anything.

145
00:11:59,066 --> 00:12:07,895
A miscommunication then sent those same fighters flying the wrong way entirely, out over the Atlantic Ocean, away from Washington, before they were redirected.

146
00:12:09,033 --> 00:12:18,201
Investigators found a subpoenaed email from the officer in charge that morning, explaining, years later, why the false version had been told to Congress in the first place.

147
00:12:18,201 --> 00:12:21,803
It's easiest to explain the scramble against American 77, he wrote.

148
00:12:21,803 --> 00:12:28,024
It takes a lot of time to explain you're scrambling fighters against a plane heading away from the target.

149
00:12:29,166 --> 00:12:31,719
One more claim didn't survive scrutiny either.

150
00:12:31,719 --> 00:12:40,471
A pilot from a separate Air National Guard squadron gave a detailed, dramatic account of personally locating Flight 93 and preparing to ram it.

151
00:12:40,471 --> 00:12:44,848
Commission investigators found his own squadron was not airborne until ten thirty-eight.

152
00:12:44,848 --> 00:12:47,765
Thirty-five minutes after the plane had already crashed.

153
00:12:48,899 --> 00:12:53,856
When investigators confronted him with the timeline, he didn't explain the discrepancy.

154
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:55,508
He left the meeting.

155
00:12:56,633 --> 00:13:00,109
To be precise about what this is, and is not.

156
00:13:00,109 --> 00:13:05,670
This is a real, documented case of an institution's public account not matching its own tapes.

157
00:13:05,670 --> 00:13:11,579
It is not evidence that anyone allowed the attacks to happen, or intentionally let Flight 93 through.

158
00:13:11,579 --> 00:13:18,184
The people at the radar screens that morning were improvising, in good faith, against something nobody had trained for.

159
00:13:19,333 --> 00:13:22,024
Chapter nine. What actually could have stopped it.

160
00:13:23,166 --> 00:13:27,636
So could any fighter, anywhere, actually have reached Flight 93 in time?

161
00:13:27,636 --> 00:13:34,712
The military's own radar center, Neads, first heard the flight had even been hijacked at ten-oh-seven in the morning.

162
00:13:35,833 --> 00:13:38,987
Flight 93 crashed at ten-oh-three. Four minutes earlier.

163
00:13:38,987 --> 00:13:44,114
The military learned Flight 93 existed after it was already on the ground.

164
00:13:45,233 --> 00:13:55,437
The order authorizing a shootdown, worked out between Vice President Cheney and President Bush in the White House shelter, wasn't even communicated to the military's command center until ten thirty-one.

165
00:13:55,437 --> 00:13:57,138
Twenty-eight minutes after the crash.

166
00:13:57,138 --> 00:14:00,880
And it never reached the fighter pilots over Washington at all.

167
00:14:01,999 --> 00:14:05,033
The Commission's own report addresses the military's claim directly.

168
00:14:05,033 --> 00:14:09,752
Norad officials have maintained that they would have intercepted and shot down United 93.

169
00:14:09,752 --> 00:14:12,448
The Commission's answer: we are not so sure.

170
00:14:13,566 --> 00:14:23,384
And that is also why a persistent rumor, that a military jet shot Flight 93 down, or that no real plane crashed there at all, does not hold up.

171
00:14:23,384 --> 00:14:26,431
No missile fragments were ever found in the wreckage.

172
00:14:26,431 --> 00:14:30,493
No fighter was ever close enough, briefed enough, or authorized in time.

173
00:14:31,633 --> 00:14:36,014
The debris pattern matches a high speed crash, not an explosive strike.

174
00:14:36,014 --> 00:14:41,126
The flight recorder data matches an uncontrolled dive after a struggle, not an intercept.

175
00:14:41,126 --> 00:14:44,778
And all forty-four people aboard were positively identified through DNA.

176
00:14:44,778 --> 00:14:48,064
Every piece of physical evidence points the same direction.

177
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:50,687
The Commission closes with this.

178
00:14:50,687 --> 00:14:55,150
We are sure that the nation owes a debt to the passengers of United 93.

179
00:14:55,150 --> 00:15:01,399
Their actions saved the lives of countless others, and may have saved either the Capitol or the White House from destruction.

180
00:15:02,533 --> 00:15:08,646
So what stays with me about this one isn't the hijackers, or even the confusion in the sky above Washington that morning.

181
00:15:09,766 --> 00:15:20,450
It's that the only reason we know any of this, the only reason there's a black box worth breaking down at all, is that forty people picked up a phone, learned the worst, and decided anyway.

182
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:23,666
No fighter jet saved that flight.

183
00:15:23,666 --> 00:15:27,799
No general, no order, no chain of command got there in time.

184
00:15:27,799 --> 00:15:34,687
The passengers of United 93 saved it, with a vote, a rush down a narrow aisle, and about ninety seconds.

185
00:15:35,833 --> 00:15:37,435
This has been United 93.

186
00:15:37,435 --> 00:15:50,256
If a black box capturing an institution's own confusion is the kind of story you came here for, wait until you see how one hidden piece of software doomed two more airliners full of people who never knew it existed.

187
00:15:50,256 --> 00:15:56,026
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, for the true story behind every disaster, told from the black box out.

